The emulator spike's goldens
============================

A held session keeps its screen in x/vt, and a viewer draws what it is sent
with xterm. The tests in cmd/rota/hold check that the two agree: for each
capture in cmd/rota/hold/testdata/vt, x/vt's final screen must be xterm's,
cell for cell, and so must the screen x/vt's repaint draws in xterm.

xterm's side is golden.mjs, run on @xterm/headless 5.5.0, the same release as
the xterm.js the web page serves (api/assets/xterm), with
@xterm/addon-unicode11 0.8.0, the addon released beside it, active. xterm's
own widths are Unicode 6's, where an emoji such as ✅ takes one column; real
terminals and x/vt give it two, as Unicode 11 does. On the other captures
the two draw the same screen, byte for byte. golden.mjs
writes its output as <name>.xterm.json beside each capture. The packages
themselves are never committed: they are fetched, checked, into a directory
of their own, and used from there.

What is in a capture directory
------------------------------

  <name>.bin                what a program wrote to an 80x24 terminal
  <name>.xterm.json         xterm's screen after <name>.bin
  <name>.repaint.bin        the holder's repaint of x/vt's screen
  <name>.repaint.xterm.json xterm's screen after <name>.repaint.bin
  sample.go, sample.ansi    what vim and less were shown

The captures are vim (stopped before it quits), vim-quit, less, the fake CLI
(fake) and the fake CLI printing emoji, CJK and combining marks (emoji).
TestCaptureVT records them; it runs vim and less with HOME in a temporary
directory and -u NONE, so nobody's own settings reach a capture.

Each .xterm.json starts with the sha256 of the .bin it was made from, and the
tests check it, so a golden left over from an older capture or repaint fails
instead of passing on a screen nobody drew.

Not covered: x/vt measures a whole grapheme cluster, so ✔️ with its
variation selector, a ZWJ sequence such as 👩‍💻 and a flag are each one cell
two columns wide, as in terminals that do the same. xterm 5.5 measures each
code point, even with Unicode 11: ✔️ takes one column, 👩‍💻 two cells of two,
and a flag two cells of one.

xterm 5.5 cannot report the mouse encoding or the kitty keyboard flags, so the
tests check those two for fake.bin against the values its script set.

To fetch @xterm/headless 5.5.0 and @xterm/addon-unicode11 0.8.0
---------------------------------------------------------------

1. Read the registry metadata and take each version's dist.integrity:
     for p in headless:5.5.0 addon-unicode11:0.8.0; do
       curl -s https://registry.npmjs.org/@xterm/${p%%:*} | python3 -I -c \
         "import json,sys; print(json.load(sys.stdin)['versions'][sys.argv[1]]['dist']['integrity'])" ${p#*:}
     done
   They are
     sha512-5xXB7kdQlFBP82ViMJTwwEc3gKCLGKR/eoxQm4zge7GPBl86tCdI0IdPJjoKd8mUSFXz5V7i/25sfsEkP4j46g==
     sha512-LxinXu8SC4OmVa6FhgwsVCBZbr8WoSGzBl2+vqe8WcQ6hb1r6Gj9P99qTNdPiFPh4Ceiu2pC8xukZ6+2nnh49Q==
2. Fetch the tarballs into a fresh directory and check each against its
   sha512 before opening it:
     dir=$(mktemp -d) && cd "$dir" &&
       curl -sO https://registry.npmjs.org/@xterm/headless/-/headless-5.5.0.tgz &&
       curl -sO https://registry.npmjs.org/@xterm/addon-unicode11/-/addon-unicode11-0.8.0.tgz &&
       for f in headless-5.5.0.tgz addon-unicode11-0.8.0.tgz; do
         python3 -I -c "import base64,hashlib,sys;\
           print('sha512-'+base64.b64encode(hashlib.sha512(open(sys.argv[1],'rb').read()).digest()).decode())" $f
       done
3. Only when they match, unpack them there:
     for p in headless:headless-5.5.0 addon-unicode11:addon-unicode11-0.8.0; do
       mkdir -p node_modules/@xterm/${p%%:*} &&
         tar -xzf ${p#*:}.tgz -C node_modules/@xterm/${p%%:*} --strip-components=1
     done
   Neither package has dependencies, so there is nothing else to install.

To make the goldens
-------------------

From the repository root, with dir from step 2:

  ROTA_VT_CAPTURE=1 go -C cmd/rota test -count=1 -run TestCaptureVT ./hold/
  export XTERM_HEADLESS="$dir/node_modules/@xterm/headless"
  export XTERM_UNICODE11="$dir/node_modules/@xterm/addon-unicode11"
  for n in vim vim-quit less fake emoji; do
    node scripts/vt/golden.mjs cmd/rota/hold/testdata/vt/$n.bin 80 24 \
      >cmd/rota/hold/testdata/vt/$n.xterm.json
  done

The repaints come from the holder's own code, so they are made by the test
that checks them, and their goldens after them:

  ROTA_VT_GOLDEN=1 go -C cmd/rota test -count=1 -run TestRepaintReproducesTheScreen ./hold/
  for n in vim vim-quit less fake emoji; do
    node scripts/vt/golden.mjs cmd/rota/hold/testdata/vt/$n.repaint.bin 80 24 \
      >cmd/rota/hold/testdata/vt/$n.repaint.xterm.json
  done

and the test is run again without ROTA_VT_GOLDEN to check them. A capture
that changes is a reason to read the new goldens' diff before committing it:
the tests compare against whatever is committed.
